<% @page_description = "Smartfire terms of service: the open-source software license and the rules for using a self-hosted workspace." %>

<h1>Terms of Service</h1>
<p class="public-updated">Last updated: <%= PublicPolicy.effective_date %></p>
<p class="public-lede">
  These terms cover two different things: the Smartfire software, which is
  open source, and the workspace you use, which is run by its own hosting
  organization. Nothing here limits rights the law does not allow to be limited.
</p>

<h2>1. The software</h2>
<p>
  Smartfire is open-source software provided under the MIT license included with
  it (the <code>MIT-LICENSE</code> file in the repository). You may use, copy,
  modify, and share it under that license's conditions, which include keeping
  the copyright and permission notices. The license gives no warranties and
  limits the authors' liability; read the full file for the exact terms.
</p>
<p>
  These terms do not change the MIT license, and using a workspace gives you no
  ownership of the software itself.
</p>

<h2>2. The workspace</h2>
<p>
  Your workspace runs on a server controlled by its hosting organization, whose
  own workplace policies also apply to you. Access is by invitation or account
  from that organization: use only workspaces you are authorized to use, keep
  your sign-in details to yourself, and tell your workspace administrator if you
  suspect unauthorized access.
</p>

<h2>3. Your responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    Share only content you have the right to share, including the right to store
    it on the workspace's server and show it to the room's members.
  </li>
  <li>
    Remember that messages, including direct messages, and uploaded files are
    stored on the workspace's server, may remain in backups, and can be
    accessible to the people operating it. Do not treat workspace chat as
    private from the hosting organization.
  </li>
  <li>
    When you connect Google Calendar or share a Drive file, you grant the Google
    permissions shown in Google's own consent screens. Those grants are yours:
    understand who receives access before you confirm.
  </li>
  <li>
    Do not misuse the service: no unauthorized access attempts, no interference
    with other members' use, no unlawful content, and no use of integrations to
    harm other systems.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Content rights</h2>
<p>
  You and your organization keep your rights in content you post; nothing here
  assigns them to the people who build Smartfire. By posting, you allow the
  hosting organization to store, back up, and display that content to workspace
  members as needed to run the workspace. Employment or contractor agreements
  with your organization may separately govern who owns work-related content.
</p>

<h2>5. Third-party services</h2>
<p>
  Workspaces can connect to services run by others, such as Google (sign-in,
  Calendar, Drive), GitHub, and the workspace's real-time media provider. Your
  use of those services follows their own terms, including Google's terms for
  Google services. Integrations are provided as available and may change or stop
  working; neither the software's authors nor, unless they separately promise
  it, the hosting organization guarantees any integration or service level.
</p>

<h2>6. Access changes and removal</h2>
<p>
  The hosting organization may suspend or remove access, and may remove content
  or accounts, to operate its workspace or meet its obligations. Removed content
  may persist in backups or where the organization must keep it for business or
  legal reasons. See the <%= link_to "privacy policy", privacy_path %> for how
  data retention works.
</p>

<h2>7. Contact</h2>
<% operator_name = PublicPolicy.operator_name %>
<% contact_email = PublicPolicy.contact_email %>
<% if operator_name %>
  <p>This workspace is operated by <strong><%= operator_name %></strong>.</p>
<% else %>
  <p>
    This workspace is operated by the organization hosting it. Your workspace
    administrator can tell you who that is.
  </p>
<% end %>
<% if contact_email %>
  <p>Questions about these terms: <%= mail_to contact_email %></p>
<% else %>
  <p>Direct questions about these terms to your workspace administrator.</p>
<% end %>
